From 2e0d2ccca2265d735254ef6c80632d2abbff0c85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Phil Elwell <phil@raspberrypi.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:58:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] misc: rp1-pio: Don't copy back more than the ioctl argument
 size

A positive return value from an ioctl handler is used as the number of
bytes of the argument buffer to copy back to the caller. However, some
handlers return a value rather than a length - READ_HW returns the
number of bytes read (up to 127 for a 16-byte argument), and SM_CLAIM
returns the index of the claimed state machine (up to 3 for a 2-byte
argument).

In those cases the copy runs beyond the part of the on-stack argument
buffer that was initialised from the caller's arguments, leaking
uninitialised kernel stack and overwriting user memory beyond the
argument structure.

Limit the copy to the size of the ioctl argument.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Mythos 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Phil Elwell <phil@raspberrypi.com>
---
 drivers/misc/rp1-pio.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/misc/rp1-pio.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/rp1-pio.c
@@ -2018,7 +2018,12 @@ static long rp1_pio_ioctl(struct file *f
 	ret = (hdlr->func)(client, argbuf);
 	dev_dbg(dev, "%s: %s -> %d\n", __func__, hdlr->name, ret);
 	if (ret > 0) {
-		if (copy_to_user(argp, argbuf, ret))
+		/*
+		 * A positive result is the number of bytes of the arguments
+		 * to return, but some handlers return a value instead (an SM
+		 * index, a byte count), so never copy more than was passed in.
+		 */
+		if (copy_to_user(argp, argbuf, min(ret, sz)))
 			ret = -EFAULT;
 	} else if (ret == -ERESTARTSYS && hdlr->restartable) {
 		/* Pass back the progress, so that a restart resumes from there */
